-- title:
--   Primality of the branch ideal (V) in the crossing model
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a commutative ring and $\pi \in W$, and assume that the quotient $W/(\pi)$ by the principal ideal generated by $\pi$ is an integral domain. Consider the crossing model $\mathrm{UVCrossingModel}\ W\ \pi$, namely the quotient of the ring $\mathrm{MvPowerSeries}\ (\mathrm{Fin}\ 2)\ W$ of formal power series in two variables $X_0, X_1$ over $W$ by the ideal generated by the single element $X_0 X_1 - \pi$ (with $\pi$ viewed as a constant power series), i.e. the local model $W[[u,v]]/(uv-\pi)$ of two transversally crossing branches. The theorem asserts that in this ring the principal ideal $\mathrm{Ideal.span}\ \{V\ \pi\}$ generated by the element `V π` — the element of the crossing model cutting out one of the two branches — is a prime ideal. In particular the ideal is proper, so the quotient of the crossing model by it is a nonzero integral domain.
--
--   The crossing model $W[[u,v]]/(uv-\pi)$ is the local picture, at a supersingular point of the special fibre, of the two branches of a $\Gamma_0(p)$-moduli problem in the sense of Deligne–Rapoport; this statement says that one of the two branches is an irreducible (indeed integral) divisor. It is used in the determination of the minimal primes of the ideal generated by the product of the two branch elements, and in the identification of the maximal ideal of a completed local ring with an ideal generated by two elements.
